DISCUSSION = [
 ("h1", "4. Discussion"),
 ("p", "This review mapped 76 studies to ask what is known about the vulnerable brain of a patient with a brain tumor who faces surgery. The main finding is an absence. No study defined tumor-associated brain frailty, and the seven studies that used frailty at all borrowed indices built for older adults or general surgical patients. Around that absence sits a substantial body of work on the four domains we propose as its components, on candidate mechanisms, and on perioperative injury. These can be assembled into a coherent working construct but not yet into a validated one (Table 7)."),
 ("table", "t7"),
 ("p", "We propose tumor-associated brain frailty as a reduced capacity of the brain and body of a patient with an intracranial tumor to tolerate surgical and anesthetic stress. It is expressed in systemic, cognitive, neurological, and imaging domains and is driven at least in part by tumor-related inflammation and barrier injury. The definition differs from imaging-defined brain frailty and from cognitive frailty in three ways {c:appleton2020,kelaiditi2013}. It is tied to a lesion and its mechanisms, so mass effect, edema, and barrier leak belong to it. It may be partly reversible, because improvement after resection was the commonest conclusion in meningioma cohorts and appeared in some glioma cohorts. And it is defined against a specific coming challenge, the operation. These features justify a separate term, but the term stays a hypothesis. A construct earns its place only when its components can be measured and shown to predict outcome beyond what age and tumor grade already tell the surgeon, and none of the mapped studies did that."),
 ("p", "Each domain has instruments and no shared standard. The systemic indices reflect comorbidity and function more than brain reserve, and none of the frailty cohorts was restricted to glioma or meningioma, so tumor-specific thresholds cannot be derived from them. This extends the conclusions of earlier systematic reviews on heterogeneous thresholds and pooled etiologies {c:huq2022,qureshi2023}. Cognition is the best-studied domain and also the least standardized. Impairment before surgery was universal among studies that looked for it, but the direction after surgery depended on tumor type and timing. Declines clustered in glioma cohorts and often resolved, whereas meningioma cohorts mostly improved, which suggests that part of the preoperative deficit is a reversible tumor effect. The choice of instrument matters, because one study found normal screening scores in patients whose detailed testing and lesion mapping showed specific deficits {c:s56}. Neurological outcome was recorded with nine different instruments or definitions, and the NANO scale, which offers a structured alternative, appeared only in the supplementary stratum {c:nayak2017}. Imaging was abundant but never converted into a frailty metric. Markers that are well established in stroke, such as leukoaraiosis and atrophy {c:appleton2020}, were absent from the mapped set. So was temporal muscle thickness, a muscle-based measure linked to survival in glioblastoma {c:furtner2019}. Both are candidates for a CT or MRI component."),
 ("p", "Whether neuroinflammation reaches beyond malignant tumors can be answered only in part. External reviews describe macrophage-rich immune infiltrates in meningioma that track edema {c:domingues2016,haslund2022}, and the mapped meningioma studies show injury and vulnerability in the form of S100B release, new diffusion-weighted lesions, and cognitive deficits before surgery. None of them measured a cytokine, and no core study compared inflammatory markers between meningioma and glioma. The cytokine data that exist, from glioma tissue, cerebrospinal fluid, and microdialysate, relate to tumor grade and early postoperative kinetics and not to frailty. The similar vulnerability phenotype in benign and malignant tumors is therefore a reason to test a shared mechanism. It is not evidence that one exists. Delirium risk was higher with glioma or malignant tumors in five of six studies, which suggests that the shared phenotype may differ in size between tumor types."),
 ("p", "The biomarker evidence needs careful reading. S100B, neuron-specific enolase, GFAP, tau, and neurofilament light chain show that injury occurred, and their kinetics differ, with S100B acting early and neurofilament light chain showing a late second peak {c:s10,s15}. GFAP is complicated in glioma by release from tumor cells {c:s23}. Serial sampling to day 10 and a panel that pairs an early astrocytic marker with a late axonal marker therefore look more informative than a single sample, as reviews of serial sampling and of neurofilament biomarkers also suggest {c:thelin2017,khalil2018}. Trials used these markers as surrogates for neuroprotection, and in the mapped studies the link to clinical benefit was weak. Lower S100B with magnesium was not accompanied by a functional difference {c:s53}, and lidocaine reduced cerebral oxygen extraction without a cognitive benefit at six months {c:s55}. Lower marker levels do not by themselves show protection."),
 ("p", "The two-hit model organizes these findings, with the tumor as the first hit and perioperative brain injury as the second (Figure 5). New diffusion-weighted lesions followed 18.8% to 45.7% of resections, including about a third of meningioma operations, injury markers rose in every study that followed them, and delirium affected 4.2% to 35% of patients. Preoperative state predicted the postoperative course in several designs. The model borrows biological plausibility from studies of microglial priming, in which a brain already exposed to inflammation responds more strongly to a later systemic challenge {c:perry2014,cunningham2013}. That literature concerns neurodegeneration and not tumors, and we do not claim it applies. What the mapped evidence cannot yet show is the interaction, meaning whether the same perioperative injury does more harm in a brain that is already vulnerable. A direct test would enroll patients with a defined tumor type and profile the four domains before surgery. It would sample injury and inflammatory markers serially, obtain early diffusion-weighted imaging, and follow cognition. The interaction between preoperative profile and injury would be the primary analysis. Modifiable second-hit factors such as anesthetic technique, hemodynamic management, and frailty-directed rehabilitation showed favorable signals in small trials, mostly with surrogate endpoints, and these should be studied inside that design."),
 ("p", "The instrument-focused stratum matters for Indonesia in particular. No core study came from Indonesia, and none used MoCA-INA or NANO-Ina. The Indonesian studies we found were cross-sectional, with 30 to 83 participants each, and measured agreement, score distributions, and sensitivity to short-term change {c:angelita2026,bangun2018,rambe2017,wonggo2025}. MoCA-INA scores fell after a 24-hour on-call period {c:wonggo2025}, which suggests that the instrument can register brief changes in cognition. Its mean scores were lower than MMSE scores in older adults {c:rambe2017} and in patients with brain tumors {c:bangun2018}, as the greater sensitivity reported for the parent MoCA would predict {c:nasreddine2005,olson2008}. Different cut-offs prevent a direct comparison of impairment rates. Responsiveness to craniotomy and the ability of either instrument to separate the first hit from the second remain untested, and a perioperative validation of both in Indonesian neuro-oncology cohorts is a concrete next step."),
 ("p", "A scoping review maps evidence. It does not appraise quality, estimate effects, or replace a systematic review or a narrative review written to a fixed argument. Readers who need effect estimates for frailty indices should turn to the existing meta-analyses {c:aghajanian2023}. The structure we offer here, with explicit domains, tumor strata, and counts that can be traced to a charting form, is meant to make a fuller later review easier. Three of the seven review questions ended partly answered. This is an acceptable and informative scoping result, because a question that the literature cannot answer is a finding that directs future work, and reporting it plainly is more defensible than stretching weak evidence to fit {c:munn2018,pollock2023}."),
 ("p", "Several limitations apply. We did not appraise risk of bias, and the evidence level reflects design only, so a level 2 study may still be at high risk of bias. The direction-of-effect tallies rest on the authors' own group-level conclusions and on our coding, and other reviewers might classify borderline studies differently, which the supplementary charting form allows them to check. More than half of the core studies sampled mixed tumor types without stratified results, which limits conclusions by tumor type. We retained two trial protocols and one preprint, which add to the map of ongoing work but not to outcome counts. Screening was limited to English-language reports, the last search was run on 22 March 2026, and Google Scholar searches are difficult to reproduce. The instrument-focused stratum was added after charting began, and two of its studies enrolled participants without tumors. Finally, we did not search for studies of general brain frailty outside neuro-oncology, so the contrast we draw with imaging-defined brain frailty rests on external sources."),
 ("p", "Studies should define and test a brain-specific vulnerability profile in single-tumor cohorts, starting with glioma and meningioma. A core outcome set should replace the nine neurological definitions and the many cognitive tests, with MoCA-based screening, a domain-specific test, and NANO as obvious candidates. Biomarker work should add cytokine measurement to injury markers and should compare meningioma with glioma. Imaging studies should test brain frailty markers and muscle measures on routine preoperative scans. And the interaction between the two hits should be the primary question of at least one prospective cohort."),
 ("h1", "5. Conclusions"),
 ("p", "The mapped literature does not define tumor-associated brain frailty, but it supplies the parts of a definition. Systemic frailty, cognition, neurological function, and imaging each carry evidence of vulnerability before surgery, and perioperative brain injury, seen as new ischemic lesions, marker release, and delirium, adds a second hit in a large share of operations. Neuroinflammation is documented in glioma and is a plausible but untested shared mechanism in meningioma. None of the 70 core studies used MoCA-INA or NANO-Ina, which have only cross-sectional Indonesian data. The evidence is observational and heterogeneous, and the interaction between the two hits is untested. Prospective cohorts that pair preoperative domain profiling with serial injury and inflammatory markers are needed to turn this working construct into a measurable and testable one."),
]

